## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to design end-to-end tests for offline-first collaborative applications—a common pattern in modern productivity tools. It evaluates understanding of multi-client synchronization, conflict resolution strategies, and proper test isolation.

## Key Concepts
- **Offline-first syncing and eventual consistency**: Understanding how data propagates when clients reconnect
- **Conflict resolution strategies**: CRDT (Conflict-free Replicated Data Types) ensures deterministic merging; last-writer-wins uses timestamps
- **Multi-client test isolation**: Using separate browser contexts to simulate concurrent users
- **Network simulation**: Leveraging tools like Playwright's network throttling to emulate offline/online transitions
- **Per-note permissions and presence**: Validating access control and real-time user indicators

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright setup with two isolated contexts
const { chromium } = require('playwright');

const browser = await chromium.launch();
const context1 = await browser.newContext();
const context2 = await browser.newContext();

const user1 = await context1.newPage();
const user2 = await context2.newPage();

// Seed shared note
const noteId = await createSharedNote(user1, 'Test Note');
await user2.goto(`/notes/${noteId}`);

// Take user1 offline
await context1.setOffline(true);

// User1 edits offline
await user1.fill('.editor', 'User1 offline edit');

// User2 edits online
await user2.fill('.editor', 'User2 online edit');

// Restore connectivity
await context1.setOffline(false);

// Verify sync and conflict resolution
const finalContent = await user1.textContent('.editor');
console.log('Final content:', finalContent);
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design realistic E2E checkout scenarios under network stress, locale shifts, and stock race conditions. It validates understanding of deterministic mocks, proper data isolation, and robust failure handling.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end data seeding with deterministic mocks
- Real-time UI updates across viewports
- Locale and region switching with discount persistence
- Race conditions: stock changes during checkout
- Failure handling, rollback mechanisms, and idempotency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright E2E skeleton
test('checkout with promo code', async ({ page }) => {
  await seedTestData({ locale: 'en-US', promo: 'SAVE20' });
  await page.goto('/cart');

  // Apply promo code
  await page.getByLabel('Promo Code').fill('SAVE20');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Apply' }).click();

  // Verify discount applied
  await expect(page.locator('.discount')).toHaveText('-$20.00');
  await expect(page.locator('.total')).toHaveText('$80.00');

  // Mobile viewport
  await page.setViewportSize({ width: 375, height: 812 });
  await expect(page.locator('.total')).toHaveText('$80.00');

  // Switch locale
  await page.getByLabel('Language').selectOption('de-DE');
  await expect(page.locator('.discount')).toHaveText('-€18,00');

  // Checkout with stock race condition
  await page.goto('/checkout');
  await mockInventoryDecrement();
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Pay' }).click();
  await expect(page.locator('.error')).toContainText('insufficient stock');
});
```

